      "revision": 110552318,
      "text": "ルイボス はマメ亜科のアスパラトゥス属の一種である。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Rooibos」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rooibos",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1371970483,
      "text": "Rooibos, or Aspalathus linearis, is a broom-like member of the plant family Fabaceae that grows in South Africa's Fynbos biome. The leaves are used to make a caffeine-free herbal infusion that has been consumed in Southern Africa for generations. Internationally since the early 2000s, rooibos became more commonly consumed, possibly due to its earthy flavour and aroma similar to those of yerba mate or tobacco."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Rooibos」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rooibos#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1371970483,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Rooibos is farmed exclusively in a 60,000 ha (150,000 acres) area in the Cederberg region of South Africa, some 155 mi (249 km) north of Cape Town. The rooibos industry is a major employer during the summer months of harvesting. Workers cut the bushes, which are initially green, then lay them in the sun to dry, a process turning the rooibos leaves into a dark red color. After it has been processed and sterilized, rooibos is ready for local markets or export.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
